.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt SSH3-SERVER 8
.Os ssh3-c
.Sh NAME
.Nm ssh3-server
.Nd SSH3 server: user sessions and forwards over HTTP/3 (QUIC)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl c Ar cert.pem
.Fl h | k Ar keyfile
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl o Ar option
.Op Fl l Ar addr Ns Op Cm \&: Ns Ar port
.Op Fl A Ar alpn
.Op Fl d Ar dg_size
.Op Fl i Ar idle_ms
.Op Fl L Ar level
.Op Fl P Ar pwfile
.Op Fl K Ar authorized_keys
.Op Fl H Ar dir
.Op Fl -sftp-jail Cm chroot | lexical
.Op Fl -url-path Ar path
.Op Fl -enable-password-login Op Cm on | off
.Op Fl -password-system Cm on | off
.Op Fl -oidc-issuer Ar url
.Op Fl -oidc-audience Ar id
.Op Fl -oidc-jwks-ttl Ar seconds
.Op Fl -run-as-user Cm on | off
.Op Fl -max-unauth-conversations Ar n
.Op Fl -max-password-failures Ar n
.Op Fl -password-lockout-seconds Ar s
.Op Fl -permit-open Ar spec
.Op Fl -permit-listen Ar spec
.Op Fl -gateway-ports Cm no | yes | clientspecified
.Op Fl -help
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
speaks the SSH3 transport of RFC 9220: one QUIC (UDP) listener carries
an HTTP/3 extended CONNECT to the
.Pa /ssh3-term
route (overridable with
.Fl -url-path ) ,
and every subsequent bidirectional stream is an SSH3 channel.
Shell and exec sessions, direct-tcp and direct-udp forwards, SFTP and
forward-agent bridges ride those channels; QUIC datagrams carry the
direct-udp control prefix.
The auth chain answers the CONNECT verdict: OIDC id_tokens
.Pq Fl -oidc-issuer ,
Bearer JWTs signed by
.Pq Fl K
authorized_keys keys (the conversation binds through the TLS
.Ev EXPORTER-SSH3
exporter), or Basic credentials against
.Pq Fl P
the password file and, when the system verifier is wired
.Pq Fl -password-system ,
the unix accounts; the password login rides on by default
.Pq the PasswordAuthentication yes of the sshd canon ,
the root account never passes the Basic step
.Pq the PermitRootLogin prohibit-password analog .
Without any
.Fl K
the Bearer JWTs verify against the default identities chain of the
session user (see
.Sx FILES ) .
Every response, the refusals included, carries the
.Cm server
field with the protocol version the clients parse.
.Pp
Logs are one-liners on stderr (journald-friendly); the log file of
.Ev SSH3_LOG_FILE
is skipped when the process runs under systemd
.Pq Ev INVOCATION_ID .
.Pp
With no
.Fl l
and no
.Fl p ,
.Nm
serves the default dual-stack listener
.Pq Li [::]:443 ;
under a systemd socket-activated unit the passed fds serve instead,
see
.Sx SOCKET ACTIVATION .
The command-line shapes of
.Xr sshd 8
.Po
.Fl h ,
.Fl c ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl o
.Pc
are served where the functionality matches, the ssh3-specific options
follow them.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-enable-password-login"
.It Fl c Ar cert.pem
TLS certificate, PEM (required).
.It Fl h Ar keyfile
The host key: the TLS private key, PEM (required).
The
.Xr sshd 8
.Fl h
hostkey shape, the alias of
.Fl k
(the QUIC listener serves the TLS pair, the private key is its
closest host-key sense).
.It Fl k Ar key.pem
TLS private key, PEM (required); the alias of
.Fl h .
.It Fl p Ar port
Listen port, the digits only, 1 to 65535 (the ephemeral 0 stays a
.Fl l
addr:0 form).
Re-ports the default listener
.Pq Li [::]:443 -> Li [::]:port
and every bare
.Fl l
address
.Pq Fl l Ar addr -> Fl l Ar addr Ns Cm \&: Ns Ar port ;
the explicit port of
.Fl l Ar addr Ns Cm \&: Ns Ar port
wins.
Under socket activation
.Pq Ev LISTEN_FDS
the passed fd carries its own port and
.Fl p
is ignored (a warning is logged).
.It Fl o Ar option
An
.Xr sshd_config 5 Ns -style
option
.Li Key=Value ,
repeatable; the keys and the boolean values compare
case-insensitively.
The supported set:
.Bl -tag -width "AuthorizedKeysFile"
.It Cm PermitOpen Ns = Ns Ar spec | any | none
The forward dial policy, the
.Fl -permit-open
spec shape.
The special value
.Cm any
keeps the dial unrestricted, the special value
.Cm none
refuses every dial; an
.Fl o
value overrides the
.Ev SSH3_PERMIT_OPEN
default.
.It Cm PermitListen Ns = Ns Ar spec | any | none
The reverse bind policy, the same shapes, the
.Fl -permit-listen
analog.
.It Cm GatewayPorts Ns = Ns Cm no | yes | clientspecified
The reverse bind policy (the
.Cm y | n
aliases ride), the
.Fl -gateway-ports
analog.
.It Cm MaxStartups Ns = Ns Ar n
The cap of the CONNECTs sitting between the arrival and the auth
verdict, the
.Fl -max-unauth-conversations
analog; 0 is the kill-switch.
The
.Li start:rate:full
form of
.Xr sshd_config 5
is not supported: the daemon names it and stops.
.It Cm AuthorizedKeysFile Ns = Ns Ar path Ns Oo Ar path ... Oc
The authorized_keys chain, the paths split on the spaces, the files
walk in the given order (the repeatable
.Fl K
table).
The
.Cm %u
token in a path substitutes the session username, the
.Cm %h
token its home directory (the passwd entry); a path without tokens
is one file for every user.
.It Cm ChrootDirectory Ns = Ns Ar dir
The SFTP jail root override (the
.Fl H
alias).
Simplified: one global value, the per-user
.Cm Match
is not supported (a one-time warning says so at the startup).
.El
An unknown option names the supported set and stops the daemon.
.It Fl l Ar addr Ns Oo Cm \&: Ns Ar port Oc
UDP listen address:
.Li a.b.c.d Ns Oo Cm \&: Ns Ar port Oc
or the bracketed IPv6 form
.Li [v6] Ns Oo Cm \&: Ns Ar port Oc .
A bare address without the port takes the
.Fl p
port or the 443 default (the
.Xr sshd 8
ListenAddress shape).
The
.Li [::]
bind serves both address families (IPV6_V6ONLY=0, the dual-stack
shape of the reference
.Fl -bind
default); a kernel that refuses the dual mode stops the daemon.
Port 0 picks an ephemeral one.  Optional under socket activation:
when LISTEN_FDS is in effect the passed sockets serve and the
.Fl l
and
.Fl p
arguments are ignored (a warning is logged, nothing is bound).
.It Fl A Ar alpn
ALPN protocol name, default
.Ql h3 .
.It Fl d Ar dg_size
The max_datagram_frame_size transport parameter, default 1200.
.It Fl i Ar idle_ms
QUIC idle timeout in milliseconds, default 86400000 (24 h, the ssh2
idle canon: a silent session lives, a dead peer is the transport
error alone).
.It Fl L Ar level
Log level: a name
.Pf ( Ql debug ,
.Ql info ,
.Ql warn ,
.Ql error ,
.Ql all )
or a numeric s3 level.  An unknown name keeps the default.
.It Fl P Ar pwfile
Password file: lines of
.Li user:secret ;
the CONNECT passes only with matching Basic credentials AND the
password login on (the default, see
.Fl -enable-password-login ) .
When the system verifier is wired too
.Pq Fl -password-system ,
this file answers first: a user listed in both places follows the
file verdict.
.It Fl K Ar authorized_keys
Authorized_keys file, repeatable: the CONNECT passes only with a
Bearer JWT signed by one of the keys of any listed file, the files
walk in the given order.  A
.Cm %u
token in the path substitutes the session username and a
.Cm %h
token its home directory (the
.Xr sshd 8
AuthorizedKeysFile shape); a path without tokens is one file for
every user.
Without any
.Fl K
the default chain of the session user serves.
.It Fl -url-path Ar path
The CONNECT route, default
.Pa /ssh3-term
(the reference
.Fl -url-path ) .
Must start with a slash; the query part of the request path carries
the username and does not affect the route.
.It Fl -enable-password-login Op Cm on | off
The Basic password login switch
.Pq the PasswordAuthentication of the sshd canon .
The bare flag is the explicit on, the value form carries the verdict
.Pq the Cm on | off | true | false | 1 | 0
ParseBool shapes of the reference; the default is
.Cm on
(the wave 1e), an explicit
.Cm off
closes the Basic step with 401 whatever backend is wired
.Pq the PasswordAuthentication no shape .
.It Fl -password-system Cm on | off
The system account verifier of the Basic method (the parity of the
reference fork 0.1.34 password login): the CONNECT username resolves
through
.Fn getpwnam ,
the hash comes from the shadow entry
.Pq Fn getspnam
with the passwd field as the fallback of the shadow-less systems and
the verdict is the
.Xr crypt 3
check.
The default is
.Cm on
.Pq the PasswordAuthentication yes of the sshd canon, the wave 1e :
the system accounts ride the Basic method out of the box; turning it
off
.Pq Cm Fl -password-system No off
closes them to the Basic brute-force surface (the
.Fl -max-password-failures
lockout books them the same way as the file backend, the invented
usernames book nothing).
An explicit
.Cm off
kills the
.Ev SSH3_PASSWORD_SYSTEM
opt-in too.
The chain order is fixed: the
.Fl P
file answers first, the system accounts second; a wrong password for
a user known to either backend counts one failure.
The root account never reaches the backends: the Basic verdict for
root refuses before any lookup and books nothing (the
PermitRootLogin prohibit-password analog, hardcoded - the root
account authenticates by key only, the key and the JWT verdicts
serve as usual).
An unprivileged daemon cannot read the shadow: the startup logs a
warning and every Basic attempt against the system accounts fails
closed (the decline before any lookup), the file backend serves as
usual.
.It Fl H Ar dir
SFTP jail directory override (the default resolves from the session
identity, see
.Sx PRIVILEGE MODEL ) .
.It Fl -sftp-jail Cm chroot | lexical
SFTP isolation mode, default
.Cm chroot
(the sshd model, the F-04 fix): every sftp channel forks a child
that chroots into the jail root and drops to the session identity
(supplementary groups included) before any user path opens, then
serves over stdio while the parent pumps bytes; after the chroot the
path confinement is the kernel's, absolute symlinks and
.Sq ..
cannot reach outside.
A jail root that is group or world-writable is refused, and a
chroot without the root privilege fails closed.
.Cm lexical
keeps the historical in-process jail (the lexical prefix check,
symlink-sensitive under a privileged daemon); an unprivileged daemon
folds to it at startup with a one-time warning.
.Ev SSH3_SFTP_JAIL
carries the same values, the flag overrides it.
.It Fl -oidc-issuer Ar url
OIDC mode: the CONNECT passes only with a Bearer id_token of that
provider.  The discovery document and the first key set are fetched
once before the listener opens.
.It Fl -oidc-audience Ar id
The expected token audience (the client id).
.It Fl -oidc-jwks-ttl Ar seconds
Key-set lifetime of the OIDC provider, default 3600; the set rotates
in the background, an unknown kid schedules a refetch outside the
request path.
.It Fl v
Verbose logging (debug).
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl -help
Print the usage summary on stdout and exit 0 (the summary moved off
the short
.Fl h :
the sshd host key owns it now; an unknown short flag prints the
usage on stderr and exits 1).
.It Fl 3
Accepted for compatibility with the interop harness; the SSH3 route
is the only mode of the daemon, the flag does nothing.
.It Fl -run-as-user Cm on | off
Per-session setuid of the root daemon, default
.Cm on
when the euid is 0 (see
.Sx PRIVILEGE MODEL ) .
When enabled, every session with a resolvable CONNECT username drops
onto that identity before the spawn; a user without a passwd entry
answers 401 at the CONNECT, a CONNECT without a session username
keeps the daemon identity.
An explicit
.Cm on
without the root privilege logs a warning and the daemon serves as
before.
.It Fl -max-unauth-conversations Ar n
The cap of the CONNECTs sitting between the arrival and the auth
verdict (the MaxStartups analog of the reference
.Cm -max-unauth-conversations ) ,
default 100.
A CONNECT over the cap answers 503 before any auth work runs - no
user lookup, no identity-file read, no signature verification; the
slot of an answered CONNECT frees with the verdict, whatever the
verdict is.
Zero is the legitimate kill-switch: every CONNECT answers 503.
.It Fl -max-password-failures Ar n
The number of failed Basic verdicts that locks the account out of
the password backend, default 10 (the reference
.Cm -max-password-failures ) .
Only the users the password backends know (the passfile line or the
passwd entry) accumulate toward the lockout, the invented usernames
book nothing.
.It Fl -password-lockout-seconds Ar s
The lockout window of a failed account, default 60 (the reference
.Cm -password-lockout-seconds ) .
While it lasts the locked account answers 429 without the password
backends being touched; a successful authentication clears the record.
.It Fl -permit-open Ar spec
The forward target policy of the
.Cm direct-tcp
and
.Cm direct-udp
channels (the
.Xr sshd 8
PermitOpen analog): a target the client asks the daemon to dial passes
only when
.Ar spec
allows it, checked before any socket is opened; a denied target
refuses the channel.
.Pp
The spec is a comma/space-separated list of
.Li host:port
entries:
.Li host:port
(the exact host and port),
.Li host:*
(any port),
.Li *:port
and
.Li *:*
(the wildcards),
.Li *.example.org:443
(the wildcard host suffix, matching the subdomains but not
.Li example.org
itself),
.Li \&! Ns Li host:port
(the negated entry, denying its own match wherever it stands in the
list), and the bracketed IPv6 literal
.Li [2001:db8::1]:443 .
Host patterns are case-insensitive; the target compares in its
canonical form as the client spelled it on the wire (the literal IP,
before resolution).
When the matched entries carry at least one allow entry, an
unmatched target is denied; a policy of negated entries only keeps
everything else allowed.
An empty
.Ar spec
(the default, or the unset
.Ev SSH3_PERMIT_OPEN )
keeps the historical unrestricted dial.
.It Fl -handshake-timeout-ms Ar ms
The QUIC handshake timeout in milliseconds, default 120000
.Pq the LoginGraceTime 120 s of the sshd canon, the fork mirror :
a connection whose TLS handshake stalls longer is closed.
Zero or a negative value stops the daemon (the config-error shape of
the
.Fl -max-password-failures
refusal).
.It Fl -max-reverse-forwards Ar n
The cap on the live reverse
.Pq Fl R
listeners of one user, default 10 (the
.Cm maxReverseForwardsPerUser
mirror of the fork): every bound listener holds one slot of its
user's budget for its lifetime, over the cap the bind refuses with
the REVERSE-CAP-DENIED report before any socket is opened.
Zero is the explicit unlimited (the pre-1d shape), the hard
per-conversation budget of 10 stays on top.
.El
.Sh PRIVILEGE MODEL
Two deployment shapes are supported.
.Pp
Root daemon with per-session setuid (the OpenSSH/sshd and reference
Go-server shape; the default of
.Nm
when the euid is 0):
the daemon stays privileged, after the CONNECT verdict resolves the
authenticated username through the passwd database and the session
spawn runs
.Fn initgroups ,
.Fn setgid ,
.Fn setuid
and
.Fn chdir
to the user's home in the child, right before the exec; the login
shell comes from the passwd entry and
.Ev HOME ,
.Ev SHELL ,
.Ev USER ,
.Ev LOGNAME
ride the child environment.
The slave side of a pty is chowned to the session user first (the
sshd pty ownership), so a pty-req followed by the drop works.
A failed drop answers the exit code 126.
Sessions on a CONNECT without a username (no
.Cm ?user=
in the path, the OIDC identity) and sessions of an unprivileged run
keep the daemon identity; a single-identity hardening or a username
mapping is required when that is not acceptable.
.Pp
Single-identity unprivileged daemon (the P7.2 shape): run
.Nm
as a service account (the packaged unit drop-in, see
.Pa ssh3-server.service )
with
.Fl -run-as-user off ;
every session and the in-process SFTP server then run as that
account, per-user identities and /home access are not available.
.Pp
The SFTP isolation follows
.Fl -sftp-jail :
the default chroot mode forks a jailed, dropped child per channel
and the daemon never touches user paths; the lexical mode keeps the
in-process server on the daemon identity (the unprivileged runs,
or an explicit opt-in) \- point
.Fl H
at the intended jail in either model.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "SSH3_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN"
